# Response headers for static hosts that read this file (Cloudflare Workers
# assets, Cloudflare Pages, Netlify).
#
# Astro copies public/ into dist/ verbatim, so this ships with the build.
#
# What is NOT here, and cannot be: a rule targeting `*.md`. Cloudflare's
# patterns allow one trailing splat, so there is no way to select every
# Markdown twin by extension. Two consequences worth knowing before you delete
# the Worker:
#
#   - The Content-Type of a `.md` file comes from the host's extension table.
#     Cloudflare and Netlify send `text/markdown`. Some CDNs send
#     `application/octet-stream`, which makes browsers download the file and
#     makes some agents skip it. Check with:
#       curl -sI https://your-site/about.md | grep -i content-type
#
#   - The `Link: <page>; rel="canonical"` header on Markdown responses is set
#     by worker/index.ts. Without the Worker, the canonical survives only in
#     the file's own front matter, which a search crawler does not read. If you
#     serve twins from a host that cannot set per-file headers, set
#     `markdownPolicy: 'noindex'` in src/site.config.ts and add a
#     `Disallow: /*.md$` for search crawlers instead.

/*
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), geolocation=(), microphone=()

# Content-addressed: the filename changes whenever the bytes do, so a repeat
# visitor never revalidates the CSS or the fonts.
/_astro/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Machine-readable surfaces. Short freshness window: an agent reading them
# should not be handed last week's index, and they are cheap to revalidate.
/llms.txt
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=300
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8

/llms-full.txt
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=300
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
